# A `for` element takes a lock on the ITERATOR it came from, so the chain the
# loop actually runs on is held end to end: `e` holds `it`, `it` holds `xs`.
#
# Without it the element held NOTHING -- the `with` binding took the container
# lock -- so its validity rested on a lock another binding owned. Reassigning
# that binding released it while the reference was still live and the container
# was free to reallocate under it: a confirmed heap-use-after-free.
#
# The element locks the ITERATOR rather than the container on purpose. Copying
# the container lock down would make two bindings claim one path, and an
# `iterateItems` / `iterateMut` walk holds that path exclusively.
main: function is {
    xs: ListVal i64
    xs.append from: 1
    ys: ListVal i64
    ys.append from: 2
    with it: xs.iterate do for e: it loop {
        it = ys.iterate
        print "e \{e}"
    }
    with jt: xs.iterate do for f: jt loop {
        p: jt.call
        print "f \{f}"
    }
}
